{
  "abstract": "We study rational Collatz trajectories whose Syracuse exponent words consist of fixed blocks repeated in linearly growing runs. Telescoping their inverse affine branches expresses the unique 2-adic anchor as a rational linear combination of Tschakaloff values. We give a self-contained normalized-tail Hankel argument for simultaneous irrationality, using distinct local valuations to identify a unique minimal term in the Cauchy–Binet expansion. If the cycle of block centers has $d$ nonzero differences, a sufficient mean-exponent condition is $S/\\ell>[4d^2/(4d^2+2d+1)]\\log_2 3$. For two blocks, extending the tail recurrence to negative indices improves the factor to $43/57$, excluding the concrete schedule $\\prod_{K\\ge1}1^{4K}2^K$. We also prove that any rational realizing the first $K$ macroblocks under either sufficient condition has logarithmic height at least a positive constant times $K^2$, with explicit finite inequalities. A simultaneous Padé construction gives a stronger bound for $\\prod_K1^{2K}2^K$. Fixed affine offsets are allowed. A height-transport argument also excludes bounded run-length defects separated by exact baseline intervals with unbounded endpoint ratios. Seven small exact CPU audits verify coefficient identities, denominators, moment determinants, and finite-cylinder realization. The approximation methods are classical; the contribution is their explicit application and quantitative consequences for these structured Collatz schedules. No assertion about arbitrary orbit words, nontrivial cycles, or the full Collatz conjecture is made.",
